<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Celebrations and Traditions – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="color: #333; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 25%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 75%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 8th Grade (CEFR: A2.3)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Celebrations and Traditions</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I Celebrate This Tradition Because...</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Comprehend descriptions in simple texts. Reading: Summarize the main points of various nonfiction texts. Phonemic Awareness: Demonstrate knowledge of phonemic awareness by pronouncing the irregular present verbs ending in the third person singular "s," "es," and "ies."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can comprehend descriptions in simple texts about cultural traditions (e.g., "What do families do during Fiestas Patrias?" "They decorate their homes with the national flag and participate in the parades."). Can summarize the main points of texts about national festivals by using text features like headings and captions (e.g., "The text highlights key moments of the festival, including parades, dances, and speeches."). Can accurately pronounce irregular present tense verbs ending in the third-person singular "s," "es," and "ies" by producing the correct sounds /s/, /z/, and /ɪz/ in words related to cultural traditions (e.g., /s/ in "decorates," /z/ in "gathers," and /ɪz/ in "wishes").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays pictures of different Panamanian celebrations (e.g., Pollera parade, Independence Day celebrations, Carnival masks). Students look at the pictures and quickly shout out any vocabulary words they remember from Lesson 1 that relate to the images (e.g., "parade," "celebration," "dance," "mask," "fireworks," "tradition"). The teacher writes the words on the board.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to identify key information by showing a short, simple paragraph about a tradition on the board or projector. The teacher reads it aloud, highlighting specific verbs ending in -s, -es, -ies (e.g., "The community <b>celebrates</b>... a girl <b>dances</b>... everyone <b>enjoys</b>...") and explicitly demonstrates the /s/, /z/, and /ɪz/ sounds. Then, the teacher models how to quickly scan the text to find answers to a simple question (e.g., "What does the community celebrate?").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do we read every single word for the main idea, or do we look for keywords?" (Expected answer: "Keywords.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When a verb ends in '-s' like 'celebrates', does it always sound the same?" (Expected answer: "No, it has different sounds.") The teacher briefly re-models /s/, /z/, /ɪz/ sounds.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Celebrations and Traditions".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short nonfiction text (approx. 100-120 words) titled "Fiestas Patrias: Panama's Spirit of Independence," including headings and captions. The text describes how Panamanians celebrate their independence, using present simple and some past simple for historical facts, incorporating vocabulary like "tradition," "independence," "parade," "celebration," "national," "cultural," "decorate," "march," "gather," "enjoy," "heritage," and verbs with third-person singular -s/-es/-ies endings (e.g., "Panama <b>celebrates</b>," "the flag <b>flies</b>," "everyone <b>enjoys</b>," "the nation <b>unites</b>").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Comprehend descriptions in simple texts about cultural traditions and summarize main points of nonfiction texts.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Reading:</b> Students read the text silently for the first time. The teacher asks, "What is the main topic of this text?" (Expected answer: "Fiestas Patrias" or "Panama's Independence Day celebration").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False Activity:</b> Students read the text again to answer 3-4 True/False statements provided by the teacher, related to key details. For example: "Panama celebrates Fiestas Patrias in July. (False)" or "Families decorate their homes with the national flag. (True)".</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Students match a heading/caption from the text to a short descriptive sentence provided by the teacher (e.g., "Parades and Music" matches "This section describes people marching and enjoying traditional music.").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Comprehending text details, using present simple and past simple, gerunds after prepositions. Applying vocabulary: tradition, custom, parade, celebration, independence, national, cultural. Practicing pronunciation of third-person singular verbs (-s, -es, -ies).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. <b>Controlled Practice (Gap-fill):</b> The teacher distributes a worksheet with sentences extracted from the text, but with key vocabulary words or third-person singular verbs missing. Students complete the sentences using words from a provided word bank (e.g., "Panama ____ Fiestas Patrias on November 3rd and 4th." - `celebrates`). This reinforces comprehension and vocabulary use.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. <b>Phonemic Awareness Drill (Reading Aloud):</b> Students work individually. The teacher asks students to identify all verbs in the text that end in "-s," "-es," or "-ies." The teacher then writes a list of these verbs on the board (e.g., celebrates, marches, gathers, enjoys, becomes, decorates, watches). The class practices pronouncing each verb chorally, focusing on the /s/, /z/, and /ɪz/ sounds. Then, students volunteer to read sentences containing these verbs aloud, with peer and teacher correction for pronunciation.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. <b>Sentence Summarizing (Pair Work):</b> In pairs, students take turns reading a paragraph from the text to their partner. After listening, the partner must summarize the main idea of that paragraph in one simple sentence. For example: "The text highlights key moments of the festival, including parades, dances, and speeches." or "This paragraph describes how people decorate their homes for the celebration." The teacher circulates, offering support and ensuring they use target vocabulary and grammar.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Celebrations and Traditions".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Reading and Discussion.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in small groups (3-4 students). Each group receives a different short reading excerpt (2-3 sentences) related to another national or regional tradition in Panama (e.g., "Carnival in Las Tablas," "The Diablicos dance," "Christmas traditions"). These excerpts use similar vocabulary and grammatical structures. First, each student silently reads their group's text. Then, they take turns reading their text aloud to their group, focusing on clear pronunciation, especially of third-person singular verbs. After reading, the group collaboratively answers the question: "Based on what you read, what tradition is this, and what is one reason why people celebrate it?" They must discuss and agree on a group answer, using phrases like "My favorite tradition is the parade because..." or "This tradition focuses on... when people gather together to enjoy...".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during group discussions and a quick written exit ticket.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each student writes a short paragraph (2-3 sentences) summarizing the main idea of their group's text and explains one reason why people celebrate that tradition. Example: "Our text describes the Carnival in Las Tablas. People celebrate this festive tradition because it helps them feel united and enjoy traditional dances."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Comprehension of the main idea of the assigned text.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (e.g., tradition, celebrate, festive, dance, gather, united).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of the summary.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Optional, for oral component) Intelligible pronunciation of third-person singular verbs.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following questions on an index card:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most interesting fact you read about today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Name one new vocabulary word you learned or practiced today."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident do you feel now about understanding main ideas when you read about traditions?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was challenging about reading the texts today, and what helped you understand them better?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b> The reading activities effectively engaged students in comprehending texts about traditions. The focus on identifying main points and practicing specific verb pronunciations (/s/, /z/, /ɪz/) was successful. The collaborative reading and summarizing activity allowed for application of reading skills. For the next lesson, I will ensure that the speaking activities explicitly build on the details and vocabulary identified in today's reading tasks. Some students may need more targeted practice with scanning for specific information and summarizing independently. Adjustments will include providing sentence frames for summaries for those who struggle.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Next Lesson:</b> This lesson's reading comprehension of various traditions and their celebration prepares students for Lesson 3 (Speaking & Interaction), where they will actively discuss and share information about these traditions with peers, using the vocabulary and descriptive language practiced today.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>